From f32d3d6df5c47e1483737d55532ca40ab5d97a4d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ond=C5=99ej=20=C5=A0imon?= <ondrej@dorhawk.software>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 18:39:48 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 65/65] win32u: Add GDI scaling, Windows' "System (Enhanced)".

A program that isn't DPI aware is drawn at 96 DPI; on a display with more pixels Windows can
stretch the result (blurry) or, with "System (Enhanced)", have GDI draw it at the display's DPI
while the program keeps its own coordinates: text, lines and shapes come out sharp, bitmaps are
enlarged. This adds the latter, on with the GDIDPISCALING compatibility layer (Windows writes
"~ GDIDPISCALING DPIUNAWARE") or WINE_GDI_SCALING=1.

A window DC of a scaled window draws into the window's surface at its display's DPI: the DC's
world-to-device transform ends with the scale, fonts are realized at the display's size (bitmap
fonts in whole multiples, their glyphs' pixels repeated), regions and clipping are mapped, and
what the program reads back (LPtoDP, GetTransform, clip boxes, regions, pixels, compatible
bitmaps and their bits) is in its own coordinates. A bitmap compatible with such a DC has the
display's pixels. A stretch the program didn't ask for is smoothed. Layered windows aren't scaled.
A window that moves to a display of another DPI is drawn again.

Text drawn in 1x pixels (on a display that isn't a Retina one, scaled or not) is rendered for an
LCD's subpixels, as ClearType does on such a display: grey smoothing keeps to the fonts' gasp
tables, which ask Tahoma and Segoe UI at menu sizes for none.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
